  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 130 AM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Heat Advisory many areas today. Dangerous heat and \nhumidity today into Friday, a brief break Saturday, followed by more \ndangerous heat and humidity Sunday into Monday.\n\nFor today and Friday, high heat and humidity will return to North \nCarolina. Expect mostly sunny skies today with highs returning to \nthe 90s. Readings may very well approach 100 in the Sandhills. Heat \nIndex values of 100 to 107 are expected. There is only a small \nchance of a thunderstorm late today into the evening. \n\nLows tonight will be slow to fall below 80, finally reaching the mid \nto upper 70s for lows. \n\nAnother heat advisory is expected Friday when highs will again reach \nwell into the 90s. Heat index values of 102-109 expected. \n\nA weak front may slide just south of the region late Friday night \nand Saturday as weak high pressure to our north tries to reach this \nfar south into NC. Most guidance suggests that this will occur with \nthe surface flow becoming light NE Saturday and Saturday night. This \nwill drop highs back about 5 degrees Saturday, with a slight \nreduction in humidity as well. However, this will not last long as \nthe weak high pressure to our north will shift offshore Sunday \nallowing WAA to return the high heat and humidity Sunday and \nMonday. Some guidance suggests highs may reach 95-100 by Monday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Chances for thunderstorms highest Friday afternoon \nand evening, shifting south a bit on Saturday PM. Some severe \nweather possible Friday.\n\nDisturbances in the WNW flow aloft a top the wavering front over \nwestern NC will likely bring the best chance of PM thunderstorms \ntoday just to our over western NC. However, an isolated thunderstorm \nwill be possible anywhere this afternoon. \n\nThere will be a better chance of scattered thunderstorms on Friday \nafternoon and evening as additional MCVs approach during the peak \nheating and into the evening. The mid level flow will become a bit \nstronger deeper south into NC. MLCapes will be impressive (2500+) \nduring the afternoon into the evening. Damaging wind will be the \nprimary threat. \n\nThe convection chances may shift west and south of our area later \nSaturday as the outflows and front move back southwest. \n\nMore chances for PM thunderstorms appear for early next week.",
